  "clues": {
    "kinetics": "Oral bioavailability is 2.9% when fasted, and a high-fat meal raises exposure about 2-fold. It is 97.3% bound to plasma protein, and metabolism is its main route of elimination.",
    "substance": "Molecular weight 440.50. The solid is practically insoluble in water.",
    "product": "Approved under an NDA, as capsules of 70 and 140 mg, tablets of 140, 280 and 420 mg, and a 70 mg/mL oral suspension. Taken once daily.",
    "safety": "No boxed warning and no contraindications. Thrombocytopenia and diarrhea are the most common adverse reactions, at 30% or more.",
    "indication": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia or small lymphocytic lymphoma, including with 17p deletion; Waldenström’s macroglobulinemia; and chronic graft versus host disease after one or more lines of systemic therapy have failed.",
    "mechanism": "A covalent inhibitor of Bruton’s tyrosine kinase that binds a cysteine in its active site. It blocks B-cell receptor signaling, reducing malignant B-cell proliferation and survival."
  },
  "tell": "A covalent BTK inhibitor with only 2.9% absolute bioavailability when fasted, and a food effect that multiplies its exposure.",
